  - Captive portal URLs: null → Google connectivity check servers           
  | why because the device only knows about my home dns?                    

● No - the device gets DNS from whatever network it connects to (via DHCP).

  The issue is different:

  Captive portal URLs = null: The device can't check if it has internet access when connecting to a new network. Without these Google URLs to probe, Android doesn't know if the WiFi actually works. It might mark the connection as "no internet" and refuse to use it fully, even if the network is fine.

  Private DNS = off: When it's on "automatic," the device tries encrypted DNS first, then falls back to regular DNS if that fails. Having it OFF can cause problems on networks that expect modern DNS behavior.

  These settings being missing/wrong means the device can't properly validate network connectivity outside your home. At home, it works because that network is already trusted/configured. On new networks, without the ability to check connectivity, Android gets confused.

