❯ Ping fails (ICMP not supported by HTTP proxy) | explain 

● Why Ping Fails But Internet Works

  Ping uses ICMP protocol - not TCP or UDP. It's a network-layer protocol for diagnostics (checking if a host is reachable).

  HTTP proxies only understand TCP - specifically HTTP protocol messages:
  - HTTP GET/POST for web traffic
  - HTTP CONNECT for HTTPS and other TCP traffic

  The PdaNet proxy at 192.168.49.1:8000 is an HTTP proxy - it doesn't know what to do with ICMP packets.

  What Happens When You Ping

  ping 8.8.8.8
    ↓
  ICMP packet → tun0 (VPN interface)
    ↓
  tun2proxy receives ICMP packet
    ↓
  tun2proxy: "I can't send ICMP through HTTP proxy"
    ↓
  Packet dropped
    ↓
  100% packet loss

  What Happens With Real Traffic (Web, Apps)

  Chrome connects to google.com:443
    ↓
  TCP packet → tun0 (VPN interface)
    ↓
  tun2proxy receives TCP packet
    ↓
  tun2proxy: "CONNECT google.com:443 HTTP/1.1" → 192.168.49.1:8000
    ↓
  PdaNet proxy: "Sure, here's the connection"
    ↓
  Data flows both directions
    ↓
  Chrome gets its response

  What Actually Works

  ✅ Web browsing (TCP port 80/443)
  ✅ Apps making API calls (TCP)
  ✅ DNS over TCP (what tun2proxy uses)
  ✅ Streaming (TCP connections)
  ✅ Any TCP connection

  ❌ Ping (ICMP)
  ❌ Traceroute (ICMP)

  This is normal and expected for HTTP proxy-based VPNs. The logs show real connections succeeding - that's what matters.

❯ does that inhibiti anything with apps being able to get to internet? 

● No - doesn't inhibit apps at all.

  Apps don't use ICMP/ping to access the internet.

  What Apps Actually Use

  Apps use TCP connections for everything:
  - ✅ HTTP/HTTPS (web traffic, API calls)
  - ✅ WebSockets (real-time apps, chat)
  - ✅ TLS/SSL (encrypted connections)
  - ✅ Streaming protocols (video, audio)
  - ✅ Any application protocol built on TCP

  All of that works fine through the HTTP proxy.

  What Apps DON'T Use

  Apps don't use ICMP for actual internet access. ICMP is only for:
  - Network diagnostics (ping, traceroute)
  - Router error messages
  - Network troubleshooting tools

  Zero apps rely on ICMP to function.

  How Apps Check Connectivity

  If an app needs to check "do I have internet?", it typically:
  - Makes a real HTTP request to a known server (e.g., Google, Cloudflare)
  - Tries to connect to its own API server
  - Uses Android's connectivity check (which uses HTTP, not ping)

  All TCP-based, all works through the proxy.

  Bottom Line

  ICMP/ping failing = irrelevant to apps.

  The logs show real TCP connections succeeding to actual servers (117.24.14.125:443, DNS to 8.8.4.4:53, etc.). That's what apps use. Apps will work normally.

  Ping is just a diagnostic tool - useful for us to troubleshoot, but apps don't care about it.

