● How it currently works:

  1. On Boot (BootReceiver)

  - Device boots → BootReceiver fires
  - Starts Tun2HttpVpnService
  - Service becomes foreground service (always visible notification)
  - Service stays running, waiting for PdaNet WiFi
  - Notification shows "Waiting for PdaNet..."

  2. When PdaNet Connects

  - Service's NetworkCallback detects gateway = 192.168.49.1
  - Starts VPN tunnel
  - Notification updates to "Connected via PdaNet"
  - Tunnel stays active

  3. When PdaNet Disconnects

  - Service's NetworkCallback detects network lost
  - Stops tunnel
  - Service stays alive (foreground service)
  - Notification reverts to "Waiting for PdaNet..."
  - Waits for PdaNet to come back

  4. NetworkChangeReceiver (Backup)

  - I added this as a safety net
  - If service somehow dies or gets killed
  - System network change → receiver fires
  - Checks gateway, if PdaNet → restarts service

  So to answer your question:

  ✅ Service DOES run all the time as foreground service from boot
  ✅ NetworkChangeReceiver is a backup - restarts service if Android kills it

  The notification should always be visible showing either "Waiting for PdaNet..." or "Connected via PdaNet".

